[TEXT] **Found it!** Router6's iptables FORWARD chain has a critical ACL on `eth2` (the r6_r3 inbound link from router3):

```
ACCEPT tcp  eth2→*  172.16.10.0/24 → 172.18.51.1  tcp dpt:80
DROP   tcp  eth2→*  ANY → ANY
DROP   udp  eth2→*  ANY → ANY
DROP   (proto 0/ICMP) eth2→* ANY → ANY    ← 111 packets (9672 bytes) already dropped!
```

**111 ICMP packets actively dropped.** This is the fault. Router6 can reach pc11 itself (bypasses FORWARD), pc15 can reach router6 (enters on eth1, not eth2), but any forwarded traffic arriving from the router3 side on eth2 — including all ICMP replies from pc11-pc14 to pc15-pc18 — is dropped. This maps to `acl-skill`.
[TOOL] Skill({'skill': 'acl-skill'})
[RESULT] Launching skill: acl-skill
[TEXT] The ACL skill confirms the analysis. I have direct evidence on the owning device. The FORWARD chain on **router6** has these iptables/nftables rules on `eth2` (the r6_r3 inbound link from router3):

1. `ACCEPT tcp eth2→* src=172.16.10.0/24 dst=172.18.51.1 dport=80` — one narrow allow
2. `DROP tcp eth2→*` — 0 pkts (no TCP attempted yet from this direction)
3. `DROP udp eth2→*` — 0 pkts
4. `DROP all eth2→*` — **111 packets, 9,672 bytes already dropped** ← active ICMP drop

The fingerprint matches `icmp_acl_block`: router6's own pings traverse `OUTPUT` (not `FORWARD`) so it can reach pc11 fine, but any return/forwarded ICMP arriving from the router3 side on eth2 is silently dropped. The 111 ICMP packets confirm live, ongoing dropping.

